    "message": "fix(upgrade): keep the compass stamp readable by already-shipped CLIs\n\nThe key=value stamp is unreadable by every CLI already in the wild, and the\nshipped CLI is exactly what reads it during the upgrade that installs it:\n`ix upgrade` swaps in the new tree, then the still-running old process\ncontinues into its own compass block and reads the file it just wrote.\n\nThat reader is getTrackedVersion — readFileSync(...).trim(), the whole file —\nfeeding splitVersion. Measured against origin/main's own isNewer:\n\n  dist 0.3.0 vs \"source=release\\nix=0.9.4\\n...\"  -> false  (garbled print)\n  dist 0.3.0 vs \"source=release\\nix=1.0.0\\n...\"  -> TRUE\n  dist 0.3.0 vs \"source=release\\nix=1.1.0\\n...\"  -> TRUE\n\nThe major parses as NaN -> 0, so at the first 1.x release every older CLI\ndownloads ix-compass-dist over the newer bundle it just installed — #376\nreintroduced by the format change itself.\n\nProvenance now rides in semver build metadata on one line:\n\n  0.9.3+release.7f98724     release bundle\n  0.3.0                     dist download (bare, as it has always been)\n\nsplitVersion already drops everything after `+`, so old CLIs read 0.9.3 and\nbehave exactly as they do today.\n\nAlso from the same review:\n\n- `present` (index.html on disk) replaces version \"0.0.0\" as the repair\n  trigger. 0.0.0 is also what a stamp truncated by a full disk parses to, and\n  repairing on that downgrades an intact release bundle.\n- install.sh only stamps when it actually extracted the tarball. The block\n  runs even when the install was skipped, where the compass on disk can be an\n  unstamped *dist* bundle — labelling that `+release` froze it against every\n  future compass update. When we did extract, the tarball's own stamp has\n  already landed and repairs a mislabelled install.\n- New producer drift test: reads release.yml, install.sh and install.ps1,\n  renders each stamp literal and pushes it through the real parser. Three\n  producers in three languages with no compile-time link to the reader is how\n  this class of bug keeps happening. Verified non-vacuous — restoring the\n  multi-line form fails both properties.\n\n744 green. bash -n / sh -n clean, release.yml parses, install.ps1 parses.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>",
